Q: How to speedup h.265 a video by 1000x without reencoding A: <p>Only <code>-c:v</code> works for me. Using <code>-c</code> creates videos with incorrect durations.</p>
<p>For vertical videos, you will also need to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/31683689/">rotate the intermediate streams</a>.</p>
<pre><code>ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -c:v copy -bsf:v hevc_mp4toannexb -an output.hevc;
ffmpeg -r 60 -display_rotation -90 -i output.hevc -c:v copy output.mp4;
</code></pre>

Q: Can a SK_SKB program use data and data_end? A: <h2>Short answer</h2>
<p>Yes, <code>data</code> and <code>data_end</code> are valid readable fields for SK_SKB programs. The verifier accepts them and <code>sk_skb_convert_ctx_access</code> wires them to the real <code>skb-&gt;data</code> and <code>skb-&gt;data + skb_headlen(skb)</code>. Your program loads fine -- this is not a permissions problem.</p>
<p>The reason <code>data_end - data == 0</code> is that the skb is <strong>non-linear</strong>.</p>
<h2>Why it happens</h2>
<p>In the SK_SKB / strparser path, the kernel assembles L7 messages from TCP segments. The resulting skb is frequently entirely non-linear -- all payload sits in page fragments (<code>skb_shared_info-&gt;frags</code> or <code>frag_list</code>), with the linear head region being empty or near-empty.</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>skb-&gt;len</code> = total message length (linear + non-linear). Reports correctly.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>data_end - data</code> = only the linear head portion, i.e. <code>skb_headlen(skb)</code>. Often 0 for strparser-assembled skbs because all data landed in fragments.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The data exists, it's just not in the linear region.</p>
<h2>Fix: bpf_skb_pull_data</h2>
<p><code>bpf_skb_pull_data(skb, len)</code> pulls non-linear data into the linear region, making direct packet access work. After calling it you <strong>must</strong> re-read <code>data</code>/<code>data_end</code> and redo all bounds checks -- the call can reallocate the underlying buffer, so the verifier invalidates all prior pointer checks.</p>
<pre><code>SEC(&quot;sk_skb/stream_parser&quot;)
int bpf_prog_parser(struct __sk_buff *skb)
{
    bpf_skb_pull_data(skb, skb-&gt;len);

    void *data = (void *)(long)skb-&gt;data;
    void *data_end = (void *)(long)skb-&gt;data_end;

    // Now data_end - data should equal skb-&gt;len
    // Must bounds-check before any dereference
    if (data + 4 &gt; data_end)
        return skb-&gt;len;

    __u32 first_word = *(__u32 *)data;
    // ... parse L7 header ...

    return skb-&gt;len;
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Can the verdict program use bpf_skb_pull_data on the full aggregated message?</h2>
<p>Yes. The verdict program receives the complete L7 message as assembled by the parser. It can call <code>bpf_skb_pull_data(skb, skb-&gt;len)</code> to linearize the entire thing and then use direct packet access on it.</p>
<p>Caveats worth knowing:</p>
<p><code>bpf_skb_pull_data</code> is a slow-path operation -- it linearizes, unclones, and drops offloads. Cost scales with message size. Fine for control-plane or moderate-rate L7 inspection, not ideal for high-throughput bulk forwarding.</p>
<p>If you only need to inspect a small protocol header, pass just the header size instead of <code>skb-&gt;len</code>. No reason to copy the entire payload into the linear region if you only need the first N bytes.</p>
<p>The alternative that avoids linearization entirely is <code>bpf_skb_load_bytes(skb, offset, buf, len)</code>. It handles non-linear data transparently via <code>skb_header_pointer</code> internally, copying into a stack buffer. Downside: no direct pointer arithmetic, you work with copies on the BPF stack.</p>
